  "output": "## Scope check first\n\nBefore diving in: this repo isn't a typical multi-tier app with a database/ORM or a React frontend. It's the **JARVIS Obsidian vault** (markdown content + a couple of Node.js bootstrap scripts) plus one large standalone vanilla-JS single-page app embedded in `index.html` (~476KB, \"TALES OF TALLES · UFC 5 CAMP\" — a training/nutrition tracker using IndexedDB + BroadcastChannel for multi-tab sync). There's no SQL/ORM layer and no React anywhere in the tree, so I'm mapping your five categories to what actually exists here rather than inventing findings that don't apply. All line refs below are in `index.html`.\n\n## 1. N+1-style query pattern → full IndexedDB table scan per cross-tab event\n\n```js\n// index.html:3701-3714\nasync function _applyRemoteEvent(eid){\n  if(!eid||SEEN.has(eid))return;\n  try{\n    const evs=await iAll('ev');           // reads the ENTIRE 'ev' object store\n    const ev=evs.find(e=>e.eid===eid);    // linear scan to find ONE event\n    ...\n```\n\nEvery `BroadcastChannel` message from another tab triggers a full `getAll()` of the event store, then a linear `.find()`. As the camp log grows (30 days × multiple daily events), this becomes an ever-larger full-table read for a single-row lookup — the IndexedDB analogue of an N+1 query.\n\n**Fix:** use the primary/`eid` as a direct key lookup instead of `getAll()`:\n```js\nconst iGetByEid = (s, eid) => idb().then(d => new Promise((res,rej)=>{\n  const idx = d.transaction(s,'readonly').objectStore(s).index('eid'); // add this index in onupgradeneeded\n  const r = idx.get(eid);\n  r.onsuccess=()=>res(r.result||null); r.onerror=()=>rej(r.error);\n}));\n```\n\n## 2. \"Re-render\" equivalent → full-DOM rebuild on every dispatch + duplicate computation within one pass\n\nThere's no virtual DOM/diffing, so every `dispatch()` call re-runs the entire render tree unconditionally (`index.html:3723-3729`):\n```js\nasync function dispatch(t,p={}){\n  ...\n  LIVE=reducer(LIVE,ev);\n  renderAll(LIVE);        // full re-render, every single action\n  updateCountdown();\n  checkMilestones();\n  persistEvent(ev,LIVE,SEQ)...\n```\n`renderAll` (`4406-4445`) fans out into ~12 sub-renders (canvas redraws, DOM rebuilds, muscle-map SVG repaint), even for actions that touch one field (e.g. adjusting a single set's kg/reps).\n\nWorse, within that single pass, `calcOvr(state)` — a pure function over `state.arsenal` — gets computed **4-5 times redundantly**:\n```js\n// 4430  renderAll:      const rd=pront(state), ov=calcOvr(state);\n// 5080  rCamp:          $s('ov-n', calcOvr(state));\n// 5082  rCamp:          const ov=calcOvr(state);      // same value as line 5080, one line later\n// 7256  getDynamicCoachMsgs (called via rCorner → rCamp): const ov=calcOvr(state);\n```\n\n**Fix:** compute `rd`/`ov` once in `renderAll` and thread them down as params (already done partially — `rCamp(state,rd)` receives `rd` but not `ov`); pass `ov` too instead of recomputing. For the coarser issue, gate `renderAll` sub-calls by which state slice actually changed (a cheap `ev.t` switch is enough given the existing reducer-action shape) rather than always running all 12.\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities\n\n- **`getNutC` reparses localStorage on every read** (`6569-6570`):\n  ```js\n  function getNutC(){try{return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(NK)||'{}')}catch(_){return {}}}\n  function setNutC(k,d){try{const c=getNutC();c[k]={d,ts:Date.now()};localStorage.setItem(NK,JSON.stringify(c));}catch(_){}}\n  ```\n  `setNutC` calls `getNutC` (a JSON.parse) just to merge one key, then re-`JSON.stringify`s the whole thing. Keep an in-memory copy and only sync to localStorage on write:\n  ```js\n  let _nutCache = null;\n  function getNutC(){ if(_nutCache) return _nutCache; try{_nutCache=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(NK)||'{}');}catch(_){_nutCache={};} return _nutCache; }\n  function setNutC(k,d){ const c=getNutC(); c[k]={d,ts:Date.now()}; try{localStorage.setItem(NK,JSON.stringify(c));}catch(_){} }\n  ```\n- **`calcOvr`/`pront` are pure functions of `state`** but never memoized — since `dispatch` always produces a new `LIVE` reference via `reducer`, a simple `WeakMap`-keyed or last-args memo would eliminate the duplicate calls noted in #2 for free.\n\n## 4. Memory growth (no classic React-style leak, but an analogous pattern)\n\n```js\n// 3679  let _lastEvents=[];\n// 3727  dispatch(): _lastEvents.push(ev);         // unbounded, every action\n// 3711  _applyRemoteEvent(): _lastEvents.push(ev); // unbounded, every remote event\n```\n`_lastEvents` accumulates every event for the entire session with no cap, and it's fully re-scanned on **every render** by `cornerConsensus`/`getDynamicCoachMsgs`/`muzysAnalytics`/`sanjisAnalytics` (`7135`, `7251`) via `rCorner → rCamp → renderAll → dispatch`. Over a long-running tab (the app is designed for a 30-day camp, left open), this is an ever-growing array that's linearly re-scanned on every keystroke-triggered dispatch — a real, measurable slowdown over the session's lifetime, not just a one-time leak.\n\n**Fix:** cap `_lastEvents` to a rolling window (e.g., last N days, since `muzysAnalytics`/`sanjisAnalytics` almost certainly only need recent fatigue/meal trends) and rely on IndexedDB (already the durable store) for anything older, loaded on demand.\n\n## 5. Redundant computations — summary of the concrete duplicate-work sites found\n\n| Location | Issue |\n|---|---|\n| `index.html:5080` & `5082` | `calcOvr(state)` called twice, one line apart, same input |\n| `index.html:4430` vs `5080/5082/7256` | `calcOvr`/`pront` recomputed 4-5× per single `renderAll` pass |\n| `index.html:3701-3714` | full `iAll('ev')` scan per remote event instead of a keyed `get` |\n| `index.html:6569-6570` | `JSON.parse` of the full nutrition cache on every write |\n\n**Bottom line:** none of the requested \"N+1 query / React re-render\" bugs exist in their literal form because there's no ORM and no React here — but the vanilla-JS equivalents (unindexed IndexedDB scans, always-full-tree re-render, unmemoized pure functions, an unbounded in-memory event log re-scanned every render) are real and fixable with the snippets above. If you want, I can apply these fixes directly to `index.html`.\n",
